<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wall Street crash of 1929</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1929">1929</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Great Crash, Black Thursday, Black Tuesday</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wall Street crash of 1929, also known as the Great Crash, was a stock market crash in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> that began in October 1929 on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> Stock Exchange. During the Roaring Twenties much profit had gone into speculation, pushing share prices far above their value. On Black Thursday, 24 October, a record 12.9 million shares were traded, and on Black Tuesday, 29 October, some 16.4 million. The fall continued until 8 July 1932, by when the market had lost some 90% of its value. Congress responded with the Banking Act of 1933 and the Securities Exchange Act of 1934; scholars disagree about its role in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wall-street-crash-of-1929.jpg" alt="Wall Street crash of 1929" width="330" height="471" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: US-gov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crowd_outside_nyse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Wall Street crash of 1929</b> collapsed the price of sugar and brought unrest and repression.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
